{
  "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
  "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "Petitioner (Craig L. Crawford)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "We confront today the Eleventh Circuit's troubling interpretation of the otherwise clause of the Armed Career Criminal Act. Under the text and structure of the act, as well as the categorical approach that this Court recognized in Shepard and Taylor, Florida attempted burglary convictions should not qualify as they -- these types of convictions do not involve explicitly, implicitly or even inherently, a serious potential risk of physical injury to another."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0000-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 4,
        "start": 4
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: If we were looking at attempted generic burglary of a residence, wouldn't that involve conduct that presents the serious potential risk of physical injury to another?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0000-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 9,
        "start": 9
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: And what's a potential risk, by the way?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Respondent",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0000-0005",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 10,
        "start": 10
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Respondent's brief, they talked about how risk and potential and serious, some of those worlds potentially knock each other out, and I apologize for using that very word, but --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Don't you think attempted use of explosives is at least as dangerous as extortion? I mean as far as the risk of physical injury is concerned, I would think attempted use of explosives is much more dangerous to physical health than extortion."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Alito",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE ALITO: Mr. Crawford, does the record show that the facts in the PSR came from police reports rather than from a plea colloquy or someplace in the court records?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0001-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 26,
        "start": 26
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: So that literally, I take it then if someone did have a fence around the house, and I, I -- I walked from the sidewalk onto the lawn toward the fence, with the intent of getting over the fence, that would qualify then, as you understand it, as an attempted burglary under Florida law?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Souter",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0001-0005",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SOUTER: Maybe, the same point, isn't it reasonable to assume that the risk of harm in these attempt cases is characteristically going to be pretty close to zero? I mean, they're not in the house. They're just on the ladder, in the kind of examples we've been talking about."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 0.94,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0002-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 41,
        "start": 41
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Do I correctly understand, we don't need the statistics, I guess they're not available, but in your view if we did have statistics and they showed that in 1/10 of 1 percent of the category of crimes across the nation, there was this risk, that would be enough?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Mr. Crawford",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 0.93,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 53,
        "start": 52
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It seems to me that Respondent's position is taking the \"otherwise\" clause to trump the entire rest of the statute. Any crime, any felony, has a potential of violence."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Court",
      "case_id": "2006-05-9264",
      "confidence": 0.96,
      "document_hash": "b4ad20d2db449bbbc3eb0c53a95c5c3d6f0aa9a11dcddcb1499c7be50ab1da7c",
      "evidence_id": "ev-b4ad20d2db44-0002-0003",
      "kind": "procedural_event",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 54,
        "start": 54
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "The case is submitted. (Whereupon, at 11:02 a.m., the case in the above-entitled matter was submitted.)"
    },
    {
